          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"balzac-illusions-perdues-dcdfed01372002fe\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Ève’s “Je vous aime bien...” is principally a direct avowal of her love for David. In this marriage-proposal exchange it also helps enact her acceptance of a future with him, although the explicit undertaking is supplied more fully by her immediately following statement that she loves him enough to become his wife and devote herself to him. Her measured, smiling response provides no independent evidence of expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The performative force is secondary and context-dependent: the highlighted words can be read simply as an avowal, while the interrupted continuation makes the marital commitment explicit.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Ève directly addresses David during their mutual declaration of love and discussion of marriage; her utterance is interrupted before she elaborates.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"David has proposed marriage and repeatedly asked Ève to say that she loves him. Ève’s restrained declaration confirms the feelings she has already described and, within this exchange, contributes to accepting the proposed relationship. The subsequent “Assez pour être votre femme” establishes that her love is sufficient for marriage and self-dedication. The smile, emotion, and romantic setting affect delivery but do not make the highlighted words a reflex-like discharge.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je vous aime bien...”\",\"supports\":\"Direct first-person presentation of Ève’s loving feelings strongly supports T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"David asks, “Dites-moi seulement que vous m’aimez un peu,” after proposing that Ève become his wife.\",\"supports\":\"The declaration answers an explicit request for an avowal and occurs within a marriage-proposal exchange, supporting dominant T and some contextual P.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After David interrupts, Ève continues: “Assez pour être votre femme, pour me consacrer à vous...”\",\"supports\":\"Her continuation links the avowed love to becoming David’s wife and devoting herself to him, supporting secondary undertaking force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Ève responds “en souriant” and uses the comparatively measured wording “Je ne suis pas si savante.”\",\"supports\":\"The presentation is controlled and conversational rather than positively marked as an involuntary or reflex-like verbal discharge, supporting E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.91}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal, while P captures its secondary role in accepting a marital relationship. Delivery and romantic intensity remain contextual, and no residual core force requires E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"In this context, French “aimer bien” is an affirmative expression of affection whose strength is clarified by Ève’s immediately following willingness to marry and devote herself to David.\"}"
        }
